11
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:28,160
[SPEAKER_00] So you've been blogging up a storm the last couple of years and I've linked to your posts a ton in the subclub newsletter.

12
00:01:28,480 --> 00:01:33,200
[SPEAKER_00] I've tweeted out links to your blogs, posted about them on LinkedIn and commented on LinkedIn.

13
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:38,480
[SPEAKER_00] So I wanted to have you on to talk more in depth about some of these things that you've been writing about.

14
00:01:38,720 --> 00:01:44,640
[SPEAKER_00] Of course, you've had a ton of great experience at Code Academy and worked with consulting clients who you've helped with monetization.

15
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:49,120
[SPEAKER_00] And so I want to dig into some of these writings that you've done the last couple of years.

16
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:57,840
[SPEAKER_00] And one of the topics that you've kind of touched on here and there is this idea of focusing on the ROI of projects.

17
00:01:58,240 --> 00:01:59,360
[SPEAKER_00] This is a really tough one.

18
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:08,320
[SPEAKER_00] So first of all, like just tell me what you mean by that, like high level overview, and then we can kind of dig into the specifics of how you actually determine the ROI of a project.

19
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:09,200
[SPEAKER_01] Yeah, sure.

20
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:12,880
[SPEAKER_01] I think it's the thing you kind of learn the hard way as a product manager.

21
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:14,880
[SPEAKER_01] You know, I was at Code Academy for five years.

22
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:18,960
[SPEAKER_01] I saw us grow from about ten million ARR to about fifty five million ARR.

23
00:02:19,120 --> 00:02:22,320
[SPEAKER_01] You know, obviously there's a ton of factors in that, just not the growth team's work.

24
00:02:22,640 --> 00:02:27,760
[SPEAKER_01] But I think it's a lesson you learn as kind of like a young product manager that you can in theory do a lot of ideas.

25
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:33,120
[SPEAKER_01] But when you're responsible for moving metrics, you need some concept of like the headroom your projects have.

26
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:37,120
[SPEAKER_01] You can't always 100% forecast it accurately.

27
00:02:37,680 --> 00:02:42,480
[SPEAKER_01] You're on a team with six to eight engineers and a designer and a product manager.

28
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:46,800
[SPEAKER_01] Your two-week sprints probably cost somewhere in the area of $40,000 to $50,000.

29
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:53,840
[SPEAKER_01] So you kind of always have to keep a mental math back to what's the likely output of the things we're doing on.

30
00:02:54,480 --> 00:03:00,160
[SPEAKER_01] Your company literally will not be profitable ever if the output of your sprints doesn't exceed the cost of your sprints.

31
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:02,480
[SPEAKER_01] I think that concept like makes sense to people.

32
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:04,800
[SPEAKER_01] It's a really hard one to implement in practice.

33
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:13,680
[SPEAKER_01] In my experience, it's not productive to try to 100% forecast the impact of every product change you're going to make.

34
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:26,960
[SPEAKER_01] But I think the way I would think about it is if you were to divide up the work you're going to do in a quarter into the buckets of technical debt clear down, small stuff and bugs that just have to happen, and big swings.

35
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:33,680
[SPEAKER_01] Big swings are typically where things get expensive, where you're implementing like giant new features, or you're like taking a big shot.

36
00:03:34,080 --> 00:03:36,400
[SPEAKER_01] I think those projects definitely need header analysis.

37
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:43,760
[SPEAKER_01] You need like a concept of what the upside could be for all the money you're going to put into them through development.

38
00:03:44,160 --> 00:03:47,280
[SPEAKER_00] You know, a lot of people go into these big projects.

39
00:03:47,440 --> 00:03:50,960
[SPEAKER_00] I'm in the middle of one right now with my side project weather app.

40
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:58,240
[SPEAKER_00] You know, we've spent the last five months on a big swing, but honestly, I didn't put dollar numbers to that.

41
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:02,720
[SPEAKER_00] The hardest thing on the big swings is that you also don't know how long they're actually going to take.

42
00:04:03,040 --> 00:04:04,880
[SPEAKER_00] It's really hard to estimate those things.

43
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:07,840
[SPEAKER_00] Let's start with the big swings and we'll kind of work backward from there.

44
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:13,680
[SPEAKER_00] When thinking about those big swings, how do you think about putting numbers to the potential for a big swing?

45
00:04:14,240 --> 00:04:17,440
[SPEAKER_01] Throughout my career, it's always helped me to do the basic math.

46
00:04:17,920 --> 00:04:21,520
[SPEAKER_01] So it's, you know, we have an LTEV of like $100.

47
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:28,880
[SPEAKER_01] We think this feature is going to increase early life cycle usage and therefore lower churn and therefore increase LTV.

48
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:42,560
[SPEAKER_01] I still think it's good to chart out the basic assumptions of how many people do we sign up for a month, how many people activate, on which screen do we think people would see this feature, what percentage might click on it, and like of that percentage might adopt it.

49
00:04:43,040 --> 00:04:44,880
[SPEAKER_01] So that's pretty simplistic math.

50
00:04:45,200 --> 00:04:49,680
[SPEAKER_01] It's not going to show you necessarily if you're right, but it will show you if your assumptions are wrong.

51
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:56,240
[SPEAKER_00] That's a great way to think about it too, because then you're actually having to force yourself to look at the numbers.

52
00:04:56,640 --> 00:04:59,760
[SPEAKER_00] And we'll get into this later with some of your turn related math.

53
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:10,400
[SPEAKER_00] But if you're only bringing in 100 people a day or 500 people a day, you do have to understand that there is a certain amount of headroom that any one feature can get adopted.

54
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:16,080
[SPEAKER_00] And then part of that equation, I would imagine, is also, will this lead to greater conversion?

55
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:21,200
[SPEAKER_00] Do we have a hypothesis that this feature will actually cause more people to convert?

56
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:25,440
[SPEAKER_00] And then will this feature cause more people to retain?

57
00:05:25,840 --> 00:05:27,680
[SPEAKER_00] How do you think about all those factors as well?

58
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:45,120
[SPEAKER_01] I guess I think it's like a mistake I see in a lot of companies and I definitely made a ton of times earlier in my career is like when you're in a company or if you're a founder working on stuff, you feel really productive kicking a lot of stuff off in parallel.

59
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:57,040
[SPEAKER_01] Because if you have a project tracker, it gets a ton of lines that show a bunch of stuff in progress and the team feels really busy, but the easiest thing to lose sight of when you're working on a product is like projects in progress don't do anything for your business.

60
00:05:57,600 --> 00:05:59,920
[SPEAKER_01] They only start to do something for your business when they go out the door.

61
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:09,280
[SPEAKER_01] So if you have big projects, kind of chunking them up into milestones, so you ship things incrementally and start to slowly test assumptions goes a long way.

62
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:15,920
[SPEAKER_01] I've never been in a company where the first milestone going out is something people feel comfortable with.

63
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:20,560
[SPEAKER_01] It's almost always like hackier than you want it to be or it's not the full experience.

64
00:06:21,440 --> 00:06:42,000
[SPEAKER_01] And I think where that can go wrong is I think when I'm going back to my point on focus, I think you should pick points in the your life cycle or monetization or product to try to deliberately increase the effectiveness of and then you just stay focused there long enough to take a couple of shots to see if you can actually improve it across most of my career.

65
00:06:42,160 --> 00:06:49,200
[SPEAKER_01] Like the biggest wins we found were always like the third or fourth swing at something because you ship something., you realize it doesn't work.

66
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:54,240
[SPEAKER_01] And if you move on immediately then, unless you were completely off base, it doesn't work.

67
00:06:54,720 --> 00:07:07,440
[SPEAKER_01] Ideally, you have enough tracking setup where you can see signs of life somewhere or you're running an AB test and it, like, the variant didn't get totally crushed by the control, then you realize you're kind of on to something.

68
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:13,600
[SPEAKER_01] Because I think when you move on too quickly, it's like it takes your engineers a little time to learn that area of the code base.

69
00:07:13,840 --> 00:07:17,920
[SPEAKER_01] So there's a certain like knowledge depth that they pay down to figure out how to work there.

70
00:07:18,320 --> 00:07:20,480
[SPEAKER_01] Same with the designers, same with the founders, et cetera.

71
00:07:20,960 --> 00:07:24,240
[SPEAKER_00] How do you think about some of these big swings impacting Tapafunnel?

72
00:07:24,240 --> 00:07:24,400
[SPEAKER_00] Top of funnel.

73
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:34,880
[SPEAKER_00] For example, my side project WeatherOp, the big thing we've been stuck on is a feature that my hypothesis is that one, I'll be able to get some press around it because it's very innovative.

74
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:40,160
[SPEAKER_00] Two, it's a kind of feature that I think will do really well in marketing.

75
00:07:40,720 --> 00:07:46,480
[SPEAKER_00] So I think this is a thing we can finally, you know, spin up some TikTok videos, advertise it on Meta.

76
00:07:47,120 --> 00:07:50,400
[SPEAKER_00] It's a kind of feature that I think will get people's attention.

77
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:53,040
[SPEAKER_00] Now, did I do anything to validate those assumptions?

78
00:07:53,440 --> 00:07:53,520
[SPEAKER_00] No.

79
00:07:54,240 --> 00:07:55,920
[SPEAKER_00] And this is where it's easy to get stuck.

80
00:07:56,080 --> 00:07:59,520
[SPEAKER_00] But how do you think about Top of Funnel in relation to these big swings?

81
00:07:59,840 --> 00:08:01,680
[SPEAKER_01] Yeah, you can take big swings anywwhere.

82
00:08:01,760 --> 00:08:08,080
[SPEAKER_01] I think the thing I at least have tried to optimize for is like cost of learning something versus payoff.

83
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:21,920
[SPEAKER_01] If you think something could go viral or get picked up or be splashy, it's like how hacky are you comfortable being building something that can validate that without fully releasing it across many different types of entrepreneurship.

84
00:08:22,080 --> 00:08:33,760
[SPEAKER_01] Like, can you pre sell access to something is a classic way of validating an early stage assumption or could you just use mockups and test that on TikTok and not get stuck behind the whole development cost?

85
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:41,600
[SPEAKER_01] Everyone has a little bit of a different comfort level in terms of like how growth hacky you want to be to validate something.

86
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:44,160
[SPEAKER_01] If you go too far, the product feels spammy.

87
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:46,880
[SPEAKER_01] The product feels wildly disconnected from the actual experience.

88
00:08:47,120 --> 00:08:50,640
[SPEAKER_01] Everybody's seen mobile gaming ads where the actual game looks nothing like the advertisements.

89
00:08:51,120 --> 00:08:53,440
[SPEAKER_01] So I think there's definitely like a balance to strike there.

90
00:08:54,080 --> 00:09:06,160
[SPEAKER_00] The funniest thing in mobile gaming though is that once people saw all those fake ads getting so much attention, they went and started building the games that were actually the experience that the ads portrayed.

91
00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:09,440
[SPEAKER_00] And from what I understand, some of those have done relatively well.

92
00:09:09,760 --> 00:09:11,440
[SPEAKER_00] But yeah, I think it's a great concept.

93
00:09:11,680 --> 00:09:15,600
[SPEAKER_00] I mean, the tricky thing there is how much you tip your hand to your future product?

94
00:09:16,080 --> 00:09:22,800
[SPEAKER_00] I mean, have you ever done those kind of hacky validations and how do you get comfortable around not getting ripped off?

95
00:09:22,960 --> 00:09:31,200
[SPEAKER_00] I mean, I think that's a big concern for a lot of folks, myself included, that if I show this publicly, people just rip it off and then beat me to the punch.

96
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:36,080
[SPEAKER_00] I think that's relatively illogical, but maybe there's some truth to that.

97
00:09:36,240 --> 00:09:50,720
[SPEAKER_01] Like, how do you how do you balance those kind of illogical fears versus maybe there's some truth to that across the companies that I've worked for or consulted with, the ones that do the best are the ones that are the fastest and like know the direction they want to move in.

98
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:54,080
[SPEAKER_01] So to me, it's like like that is the most important thing.

99
00:09:54,640 --> 00:10:05,040
[SPEAKER_01] So companies that I've seen struggle more like they spread themselves too thin too early or they take too long trying to build something and validate the concept.

100
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:17,120
[SPEAKER_01] When I was at Code Academy, we were the leader in that little industry of learning to code at the time, which was big at the time, we saw companies copy stuff that we were doing that I knew didn't work and we just like never cleaned it up because we moved on to something else.

101
00:10:17,360 --> 00:10:28,240
[SPEAKER_01] But like there's a ton of other learn to code tools that duplicated features we built that like we saw didn't work or we saw be net neutral or like ineffective and they copied it assuming that we knew what we were doing.

102
00:10:29,920 --> 00:10:35,040
[SPEAKER_01] also testing something that we saw not work and then we just like left it live because we got distracted and we moved on to other stuff.

103
00:10:35,280 --> 00:10:36,000
[SPEAKER_01] That's hilarious.

104
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:46,000
[SPEAKER_00] So then the answer there is maybe you do that validation at the point at which you know you can deliver on it rapidly enough to not worry about that competition.

105
00:10:46,560 --> 00:10:54,240
[SPEAKER_00] And then that's really just a fundamental skill of a business, especially these days with AI assisted development and other stuff.

106
00:10:54,560 --> 00:10:59,280
[SPEAKER_00] The pace of development is just moving so quickly and the pace of being able to copy something is moving so quickly.

107
00:10:59,520 --> 00:11:03,680
[SPEAKER_00] So you just need to be ahead of that as a business or you're not going to stay around.

108
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:04,720
[SPEAKER_00] Yeah, definitely.

109
00:11:04,800 --> 00:11:08,960
[SPEAKER_01] I think like the point on why what to copy is, like, for me, like a nuanced one.

110
00:11:09,200 --> 00:11:17,280
[SPEAKER_01] It's like, I think you, the founder or the product team or whoever you are, you should be opinioning on the types of features you should build.

111
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:22,960
[SPEAKER_01] And you should not look to competitors unless you're like woefully under the baseline on what you should build next.

112
00:11:23,360 --> 00:11:29,440
[SPEAKER_01] I think once you're building a feature, you should look at the best practices and reinvent as little of the wheel as possible.

113
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:39,520
[SPEAKER_01] So if you're building like a document finder, like look at Google Docs, look at SharePoint, look at Finder in Mac, look at all the little features those things have, and those are probably the baseline.

114
00:11:40,240 --> 00:11:42,960
[SPEAKER_01] Whether you should have a document finder is kind of up to you.

115
00:11:42,960 --> 00:11:43,040
[SPEAKER_01] you.

116
00:11:43,120 --> 00:11:48,000
[SPEAKER_01] But it's like once you're committed to a feature set, just steal all the best practices from the people who are the best at it.

117
00:11:48,240 --> 00:11:51,680
[SPEAKER_01] It might not be your competitors, it might be people who do that as the core business.

118
00:11:52,320 --> 00:11:52,400
[SPEAKER_00] Yeah.

119
00:11:52,960 --> 00:12:00,160
[SPEAKER_00] It's a really good point too that you never know what is actually working and what is not working when you look at things from the outside.

120
00:12:00,320 --> 00:12:07,600
[SPEAKER_00] I see this a ton in the app industry of people tweeting, oh, this app is making 200 K a month, copy these five steps.

121
00:12:07,920 --> 00:12:11,200
[SPEAKER_00] And then for some of those, I actually know the folks who build the app.

122
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:19,440
[SPEAKER_00] And I know, you know, for the five points that this growth hacker made on Twitter, it's like, that's not why the business is being successful.

123
00:12:20,240 --> 00:12:24,560
[SPEAKER_00] Those four things are completely irrelevant and aren't what's fundamentally driving the business.

124
00:12:24,880 --> 00:12:27,120
[SPEAKER_00] It's this other thing that you just can't even see.

125
00:12:27,920 --> 00:12:40,240
[SPEAKER_00] Even though you can look now across TikTok and Meta and other platforms to see which ads are being shown aton, you don't always even know whether those ads that are shown aton are actually profitable or not.

126
00:12:40,480 --> 00:12:45,920
[SPEAKER_00] And then even if they are actually profitable for that app, would it then actually be profitable for your app?

127
00:12:46,240 --> 00:12:53,840
[SPEAKER_00] I think people lean a little too much on this, on copying the wrong things without really understanding the deeper context behind them.

128
00:12:54,240 --> 00:12:54,560
[SPEAKER_00] Exactly.

129
00:12:54,800 --> 00:12:57,120
[SPEAKER_01] I feel like that's why I kind of write my blog in this way.

130
00:12:58,160 --> 00:13:04,640
[SPEAKER_01] I get the appeal of looking at other companies and like their tools now where you can observe their AB tests.

131
00:13:05,360 --> 00:13:09,120
[SPEAKER_01] And it's like, see, they ship one thing and then another thing and they went with the other thing.

132
00:13:09,440 --> 00:13:16,880
[SPEAKER_01] And it's like, it's unclear from the outside if that's true, like if they calculated that correctly, if they went with something that wasn't actually the winner.

133
00:13:17,120 --> 00:13:20,160
[SPEAKER_01] So also, it's really unclear if that would apply to you, the company.

134
00:13:20,480 --> 00:13:27,360
[SPEAKER_01] So for me, it's more important to like look at the theory behind why these things work and care less about the exact implementations.

135
00:13:28,080 --> 00:13:37,040
[SPEAKER_00] We've chased a few rabbits here, but I did want to circle back to the other two things you talked about in this kind of RL ROI equation, one of them is the small things.

136
00:13:37,440 --> 00:13:47,520
[SPEAKER_00] And how do you think about paying back the technical debt, making small changes and the ROI of these smaller things along the way versus the big swings, which we've talked about?

137
00:13:47,840 --> 00:13:53,040
[SPEAKER_01] Yeah, I think about it kind of like an investment portfolio where you need a certain percentage in each bucket.

138
00:13:53,520 --> 00:13:56,640
[SPEAKER_01] So if you're just starting out, you probably don't have a lot of technical debt.

139
00:13:57,040 --> 00:14:03,920
[SPEAKER_01] There's probably still like a layer of technical cleanup that has to happen or shuttering old features or updating libraries and scripts, et cetera.

140
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:10,000
[SPEAKER_01] Maybe you only need like ten percent of your quarterly development time going to that, but you need some percent.

141
00:14:10,720 --> 00:14:13,440
[SPEAKER_01] Or the product starts to get stale and technically really hard to manage.

142
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:19,120
[SPEAKER_01] If you were at a giant company like Microsoft, they could be paying off fifty percent of their roadmap with technical dead items.

143
00:14:19,200 --> 00:14:21,040
[SPEAKER_01] I think it depends a lot on the face of life.

144
00:14:21,680 --> 00:14:36,320
[SPEAKER_01] I think in the kind of smaller optimizations bucket, I think it's really easy for teams to get lost pursuing just the bigger things and forgetting about basic product quality, especially in certain areas like purchase flows, onboarding.

145
00:14:36,720 --> 00:14:40,000
[SPEAKER_01] There are certain parts of the application where one hundred percent of your users go through it.

146
00:14:40,160 --> 00:14:43,040
[SPEAKER_01] And they go through it at times where they're not really sure if they're staying with your product.

147
00:14:43,360 --> 00:14:46,000
[SPEAKER_01] To me, those things should be polished to a mirror finish.

148
00:14:46,320 --> 00:14:53,440
[SPEAKER_01] So it's a thing I've talked about a couple of places, but one of the highest ROI projects we ever did at Code Academy is we rewrote all the checkout page error copy.

149
00:14:53,760 --> 00:15:02,960
[SPEAKER_01] So like if you plugged in a payment and it didn't work, making sure you got a message back that actually told you what to do and didn't just say like invalid payment method, please contact your bank.

150
00:15:03,360 --> 00:15:05,120
[SPEAKER_01] Tell them what's wrong, tell them what to fix it.

151
00:15:05,280 --> 00:15:06,800
[SPEAKER_01] If it doesn't work, send them PayPal.

152
00:15:07,040 --> 00:15:08,800
[SPEAKER_01] PayPal doesn't work, send them back to a card.

153
00:15:09,200 --> 00:15:23,360
[SPEAKER_01] It literally took like two days of downloading stuff, stack ranking the errors, rewriting it, merging it back to the code base and like maybe that, let's say, very conservatively lifted checkout page conversion like one percent, but one hundred percent of your new revenue goes through the checkout page.

154
00:15:23,520 --> 00:15:26,400
[SPEAKER_01] So it's really like you lift the whole business's revenue one percent.

155
00:15:26,880 --> 00:15:35,120
[SPEAKER_00] So for a subscription app that would be then making sure your onboarding through paywall are just incredibly dilted in.

